WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.310
Team Building.

2
00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:05.665
Day 77 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:07.415 --> 00:00:08.439
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.719 --> 00:00:19.599
A team is built through small, repeated moments, not offsite games: how people greet each other, how mistakes are handled, who gets credit, and whether it's safe to say I don't know.

5
00:00:19.879 --> 00:00:22.204
Everyone helps build it, not only the lead.

6
00:00:23.554 --> 00:00:24.407
Part 1.

7
00:00:24.687 --> 00:00:26.074
What makes a strong team.

8
00:00:27.324 --> 00:00:28.241
Step 1:

9
00:00:28.521 --> 00:00:33.663
Psychological safety: people can ask, admit mistakes and disagree without fear.

10
00:00:33.943 --> 00:00:34.860
Step 2:

11
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.956
Dependability: people do what they said, on time.

12
00:00:38.236 --> 00:00:39.153
Step 3:

13
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:43.401
Clarity: everyone knows the goals, roles and how decisions are made.

14
00:00:43.681 --> 00:00:44.641
Step 4:

15
00:00:44.921 --> 00:00:47.503
Meaning: the work matters to the people doing it.

16
00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:48.743
Step 5:

17
00:00:49.023 --> 00:00:51.817
Impact: people can see their work makes a difference.

18
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:57.647
These come from widely cited research on what effective teams have in common.

19
00:00:57.927 --> 00:01:01.639
Psychological safety is the foundation the rest depend on.

20
00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:03.864
Part 2.

21
00:01:04.144 --> 00:01:06.064
As a team member: what you can do.

22
00:01:07.314 --> 00:01:08.295
Help first:

23
00:01:08.575 --> 00:01:13.183
Review PRs quickly, answer questions in channels, pair with someone who's stuck.

24
00:01:13.463 --> 00:01:14.615
Admit mistakes:

25
00:01:14.895 --> 00:01:16.111
That bug was mine.

26
00:01:16.391 --> 00:01:17.501
Here's the fix.

27
00:01:17.781 --> 00:01:20.959
When one person does this, others feel safe to do it too.

28
00:01:21.239 --> 00:01:22.178
Give credit:

29
00:01:22.458 --> 00:01:25.487
Thank people by name, publicly, for specific work.

30
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:26.770
Include people:

31
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:28.735
Invite the new joiner to lunch.

32
00:01:29.015 --> 00:01:30.914
Ask the quiet person for their view.

33
00:01:32.264 --> 00:01:33.160
Part 3.

34
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.467
As a team lead: building the team.

35
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:37.847
Set it up clearly.

36
00:01:38.127 --> 00:01:40.069
Team charter (first team meeting).

37
00:01:40.349 --> 00:01:42.546
Agree these together and write them down:

38
00:01:43.896 --> 00:01:45.475
Here is the MARKDOWN code.

39
00:01:45.755 --> 00:01:46.907
Our team charter.

40
00:01:47.187 --> 00:01:47.955
Mission:

41
00:01:48.235 --> 00:01:51.520
Make checkout fast and reliable for every customer.

42
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:52.739
How we work:

43
00:01:55.589 --> 00:01:56.698
Opening script.

44
00:01:56.978 --> 00:01:57.917
You could say:

45
00:01:58.197 --> 00:02:02.015
I'd like us to agree on how we want to work together, not rules from me.

46
00:02:02.295 --> 00:02:07.287
What's one thing from a past team that worked really well, and one thing you never want to see again?

47
00:02:08.637 --> 00:02:09.917
Roles and ownership.

48
00:02:10.197 --> 00:02:15.168
Give everyone one clear area to own (a service, a process, the on-call runbook).

49
00:02:15.448 --> 00:02:18.413
Ownership builds commitment faster than any activity.

50
00:02:19.763 --> 00:02:21.534
Build psychological safety.

51
00:02:21.814 --> 00:02:23.158
Here is a quick table:

52
00:02:23.438 --> 00:02:24.377
Do, and Say.

53
00:02:24.657 --> 00:02:25.809
Model not knowing:

54
00:02:26.089 --> 00:02:27.027
I don't know.

55
00:02:27.307 --> 00:02:28.843
Who's worked with this before?

56
00:02:29.123 --> 00:02:30.766
Thank people for bad news:

57
00:02:31.046 --> 00:02:32.646
Thanks for flagging this early.

58
00:02:32.926 --> 00:02:34.526
That's exactly what I want.

59
00:02:34.806 --> 00:02:35.937
Invite dissent:

60
00:02:36.217 --> 00:02:37.283
What are we missing?

61
00:02:37.563 --> 00:02:38.971
Who sees it differently?

62
00:02:39.251 --> 00:02:40.851
Speak last in discussions:

63
00:02:41.131 --> 00:02:43.414
(Let others share first so you don't anchor them).

64
00:02:43.694 --> 00:02:45.337
Handle mistakes as learning:

65
00:02:45.617 --> 00:02:47.238
What in our process allowed this?

66
00:02:47.518 --> 00:02:49.054
How do we fix the process?

67
00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:51.062
Shut down disrespect quickly:

68
00:02:51.342 --> 00:02:53.838
Let's keep it about the idea, not the person.

69
00:02:55.188 --> 00:02:56.105
Part 4.

70
00:02:56.385 --> 00:02:59.500
Rituals that build the team (low effort, high value).

71
00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.094
Here is a quick table:

72
00:03:02.374 --> 00:03:04.507
Ritual, and How, and Why it works.

73
00:03:04.787 --> 00:03:05.811
Weekly wins.

74
00:03:06.091 --> 00:03:06.753
How:

75
00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:10.041
5 min on Friday: everyone shares one win.

76
00:03:10.321 --> 00:03:11.302
Why it works:

77
00:03:11.582 --> 00:03:13.374
Visibility + celebration.

78
00:03:13.654 --> 00:03:14.721
Kudos channel.

79
00:03:15.001 --> 00:03:16.963
How: kudos for public thanks.

80
00:03:17.243 --> 00:03:18.225
Why it works:

81
00:03:18.505 --> 00:03:20.979
Recognition, especially across teams.

82
00:03:21.259 --> 00:03:22.198
Demo day.

83
00:03:22.478 --> 00:03:23.139
How:

84
00:03:23.419 --> 00:03:25.723
Monthly; anyone shows anything they built.

85
00:03:26.003 --> 00:03:26.985
Why it works:

86
00:03:27.265 --> 00:03:29.334
Pride, learning, cross-pollination.

87
00:03:29.614 --> 00:03:30.425
Retro.

88
00:03:30.705 --> 00:03:31.366
How:

89
00:03:31.646 --> 00:03:33.993
Every sprint; act on 1 to 2 items.

90
00:03:34.273 --> 00:03:35.254
Why it works:

91
00:03:35.534 --> 00:03:37.518
People see their input changes things.

92
00:03:37.798 --> 00:03:39.270
Pairing or mob sessions.

93
00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:40.211
How:

94
00:03:40.491 --> 00:03:41.985
1 to 2 hours a week.

95
00:03:42.265 --> 00:03:43.246
Why it works:

96
00:03:43.526 --> 00:03:45.446
Knowledge sharing, relationships.

97
00:03:45.726 --> 00:03:46.771
Lunch and learn.

98
00:03:47.051 --> 00:03:47.713
How:

99
00:03:47.993 --> 00:03:49.849
Rotating speaker, 30 min.

100
00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.110
Why it works:

101
00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.523
Everyone teaches, everyone grows.

102
00:03:53.803 --> 00:03:54.977
Onboarding buddy.

103
00:03:55.257 --> 00:03:55.918
How:

104
00:03:56.198 --> 00:03:58.715
Each new joiner gets a buddy for 30 days.

105
00:03:58.995 --> 00:03:59.977
Why it works:

106
00:04:00.257 --> 00:04:02.731
Fast integration, a friend from day 1.

107
00:04:03.011 --> 00:04:04.313
Team lunch or chai.

108
00:04:04.593 --> 00:04:05.254
How:

109
00:04:05.534 --> 00:04:07.091
Monthly, no work talk.

110
00:04:07.371 --> 00:04:08.353
Why it works:

111
00:04:08.633 --> 00:04:09.870
Personal connection.

112
00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.180
Part 5.

113
00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:13.612
Team activities.

114
00:04:14.862 --> 00:04:16.249
In-office or offsite.

115
00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:21.499
Hackathon day: small mixed teams, a fun or useful problem, a demo at the end.

116
00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:26.473
Cooking or cricket or bowling outing: shared fun, without forced trust falls.

117
00:04:26.753 --> 00:04:29.227
Volunteering day: builds shared meaning.

118
00:04:29.507 --> 00:04:33.539
Strategy offsite: half the day on the team's direction, half on fun.

119
00:04:34.889 --> 00:04:36.148
Remote or hybrid.

120
00:04:36.428 --> 00:04:40.353
Virtual coffee roulette: random pairs, 15 minutes, every week.

121
00:04:40.633 --> 00:04:45.049
Online games: quizzes, Pictionary-style drawing games, coding puzzles.

122
00:04:45.329 --> 00:04:49.468
Show your desk or pet or hometown in 2 minutes at the start of a meeting.

123
00:04:49.748 --> 00:04:53.055
Async photo channel: weekend pictures, food, travel.

124
00:04:53.335 --> 00:04:56.343
In-person meetup once or twice a year if possible.

125
00:04:57.693 --> 00:04:59.463
Icebreakers that aren't awkward.

126
00:04:59.743 --> 00:05:02.069
What's one thing you're looking forward to this month?

127
00:05:02.349 --> 00:05:04.525
What was your first ever computer or phone?

128
00:05:04.805 --> 00:05:06.767
Best bug you've ever found or caused?

129
00:05:07.047 --> 00:05:08.647
One app you couldn't live without?

130
00:05:08.927 --> 00:05:10.869
What did you want to be when you were 10?

131
00:05:12.219 --> 00:05:12.965
Warning:

132
00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:14.845
Make activities inclusive.

133
00:05:15.125 --> 00:05:19.925
Not everyone drinks, eats every food, can stay late, or enjoys physical games.

134
00:05:20.205 --> 00:05:26.947
Mix the timings (some during work hours), the formats and the venues, and never make fun activities compulsory.

135
00:05:28.297 --> 00:05:29.235
Part 6.

136
00:05:29.515 --> 00:05:30.987
Handling team problems.

137
00:05:32.237 --> 00:05:33.581
Here is a quick table:

138
00:05:33.861 --> 00:05:35.717
Problem, and What to say or do.

139
00:05:35.997 --> 00:05:37.448
Two people in conflict:

140
00:05:37.728 --> 00:05:39.968
Meet each one privately, then together:

141
00:05:40.248 --> 00:05:42.168
You both want the release to succeed.

142
00:05:42.448 --> 00:05:44.283
Let's agree how you'll work together.

143
00:05:44.563 --> 00:05:46.291
Silos (my code, your code):

144
00:05:46.571 --> 00:05:49.813
Rotate code reviews and on-call; pair across areas.

145
00:05:50.093 --> 00:05:51.779
One person carrying the team:

146
00:05:52.059 --> 00:05:56.027
Share their knowledge (docs, pairing) and spread the load before they burn out.

147
00:05:56.307 --> 00:05:58.376
Low morale after a hard period:

148
00:05:58.656 --> 00:06:00.064
Acknowledge it honestly:

149
00:06:00.344 --> 00:06:01.837
The last two months were tough.

150
00:06:02.117 --> 00:06:04.613
Celebrate what was achieved; give real rest.

151
00:06:04.893 --> 00:06:06.429
New joiner not fitting in:

152
00:06:06.709 --> 00:06:10.869
Assign a buddy, involve them in pairing, check in privately every week.

153
00:06:11.149 --> 00:06:12.173
Cliques forming:

154
00:06:12.453 --> 00:06:16.741
Mix pairs and project groups on purpose; include everyone in decisions.

155
00:06:18.091 --> 00:06:19.030
Part 7.

156
00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:20.569
Celebrating the team.

157
00:06:21.819 --> 00:06:22.757
You could say:

158
00:06:23.037 --> 00:06:25.107
Before we close: thank you, everyone.

159
00:06:25.387 --> 00:06:28.629
We shipped slot booking in 3 cities with zero downtime.

160
00:06:28.909 --> 00:06:36.568
Special shout-out to Kavya for the load testing that caught the race condition, and to Arun for staying calm through two incidents.

161
00:06:36.848 --> 00:06:38.384
Lunch is on me on Friday!

162
00:06:39.734 --> 00:06:44.555
Be specific, name the people, and celebrate effort and learning, not only outcomes.

163
00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:46.780
Part 8.

164
00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:48.596
Signs your team is healthy.

165
00:06:49.846 --> 00:06:52.598
People ask stupid questions in public channels.

166
00:06:52.878 --> 00:06:54.691
Bad news reaches the lead early.

167
00:06:54.971 --> 00:06:59.366
Disagreements happen openly in meetings, not in private complaints afterwards.

168
00:06:59.646 --> 00:07:01.886
People help each other without being asked.

169
00:07:02.166 --> 00:07:04.278
Retro actions actually get done.

170
00:07:04.558 --> 00:07:06.819
People laugh together at least once a week.

171
00:07:08.169 --> 00:07:09.065
Part 9.

172
00:07:09.345 --> 00:07:10.732
Practice conversation:

173
00:07:11.012 --> 00:07:12.463
Setting up a team charter.

174
00:07:13.713 --> 00:07:15.739
Here is how the conversation goes.

175
00:07:16.019 --> 00:07:18.387
I'd like us to agree on how we work together.

176
00:07:18.667 --> 00:07:20.502
What worked well in your past teams?

177
00:07:20.782 --> 00:07:22.211
Fast code reviews.

178
00:07:22.491 --> 00:07:24.561
It's frustrating waiting three days.

179
00:07:24.841 --> 00:07:26.441
Blameless post-mortems.

180
00:07:26.721 --> 00:07:29.067
In my last team, people hid mistakes.

181
00:07:29.347 --> 00:07:30.649
Clear decisions.

182
00:07:30.929 --> 00:07:34.278
Sometimes we discuss for weeks and nobody decides.

183
00:07:35.628 --> 00:07:37.228
The conversation continues.

184
00:07:37.508 --> 00:07:38.617
Great points.

185
00:07:38.897 --> 00:07:44.785
So: reviews within one working day, blameless post-mortems, and the owner decides after consulting?

186
00:07:45.065 --> 00:07:45.769
Yes.

187
00:07:46.049 --> 00:07:48.972
And maybe a short weekly wins on Fridays?

188
00:07:49.252 --> 00:07:50.340
Love it.

189
00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:52.455
I'll write it up and share it today.

190
00:07:53.805 --> 00:07:55.341
That's the end of this lesson.

191
00:07:55.621 --> 00:08:00.271
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

192
00:08:00.551 --> 00:08:06.674
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

